WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.417
Normalization separates facts into different tables so each fact has a clear home.

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:07.987
Later, analytical queries reconnect those tables through keys.

00:00:07.987 --> 00:00:11.362
This slide previews that idea with a provided join query.

00:00:11.362 --> 00:00:16.797
Joins are taught step by step in Module 6, so you are not expected to write this query yet.

00:00:16.797 --> 00:00:19.242
Run it and inspect the connected result.

00:00:19.242 --> 00:00:22.272
Notice how patient, provider, appointment, service,

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:27.942
and service-line information can be combined without having stored all of those facts in one giant table.

00:00:27.942 --> 00:00:33.037
That is a major benefit of relational design: store facts in well-structured places,

00:00:33.037 --> 00:00:36.117
then reconnect them when a question requires a broader view.

00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:40.462
For now, focus on recognizing how keys make that reconnection possible.

00:00:40.462 --> 00:00:44.662
Connect this idea to an online store separating customers, orders, products, and order lines.

00:00:44.662 --> 00:00:49.177
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:49.177 --> 00:00:54.662
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:54.662 --> 00:00:59.377
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:59.377 --> 00:01:04.109
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
